X-Deja-AN: 273276239
Distribution: world
Sender: Robert.Heath@columbiasc.ncr.com
References: <iwkhgcbmgj6.fsf@bmerhe83.nortel.ca> <5u3hc1$2ue$1@newsfeeds.grolier.fr> <5uaa12$982@nebula.mpn.com>
Organization: NCR Corp., Columbia SC
Newsgroups: comp.unix.shell,comp.unix.admin,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.questions,comp.unix.sys5.misc,comp.sys.hp.hpux,comp.sys.sun.admin,comp.unix.aix


I don't think there's a consensus for a standard directory in which the
POSIX shell resides.

In the Single UNIX Specification (a.k.a. UNIX95 or XPG4 UNIX) the exact 
path of the POSIX shell is not specified.  In fact, the standard 
typcally avoids specification of complete pathnames  altogether.

	Here are two possible solutions for finding the standard shell though: 

1) If your system has a "getconf" utility then type  

	getconf CS_PATH

		or 

	getconf PATH

   which produces a colon-separated list of directories, one of which 
   should contain the standard shell, named "sh".

2) At the `C' language programming interface, you can use

   	#include <unistd.h>

   	char buffer[2048];  /* more elegant solutions for sizing are possible */

   	confstr(_CS_PATH, buffer, sizeof(buffer));

   On return, buffer[] contains the colon-separated list of directories
   one of which should contain the standard shell, named "sh".


-- 
